## Quick start

You need [asdf](https://asdf-vm.com) and [direnv](https://direnv.net). Then:

```sh
asdf install                  # installs exactly what .tool-versions pins
direnv allow                  # once; loads .env and puts .venv/bin on PATH
make run                      # serves the API on :[[ port ]]
curl -i localhost:[[ port ]]/   # -> 200, "ok"
```

`make help` lists every target. The target names, their descriptions and
their output are identical in a Go service and a Python service minted from
Mint — if you have used one, you have used this one.

## What works today

This service is deliberately almost empty. It has the full directory
skeleton, a real Makefile, pinned tooling, and a `/` that answers `200 ok`.
Everything else arrives from the template through `copier update` as Mint
ships it:

| | |
| --- | --- |
| configuration | Mint chunk 03 |
| logging | Mint chunk 04 |
| the error contract | Mint chunk 05 |
| middleware, health endpoints, the admin listener, graceful shutdown | Mint chunk 06 |
| the operation registry and a worked example | Mint chunk 07 |
| tracing and metrics | Mint chunk 08 |
| `openapi.json`, `llms.txt`, the generated AGENTS.md block | Mint chunk 09 |

Targets that are not implemented yet exit non-zero and name the chunk that
delivers them. None of them exits 0 having done nothing.

**Every directory carries a module docstring saying what belongs in it and
what does not.** Read it before you put a file there. That is what the empty
directories are for.

## Layout

```
src/[[ package_name ]]/__main__.py    composition root — wiring only, no logic
  internal/transport/http/      the ONLY package that may import FastAPI
  internal/service/             business logic; plain Python values in and out
  internal/repository/          databases and other services, behind protocols
  internal/apierror/            the error taxonomy and its status mapping
  internal/config/              the ONLY package that may read an env var
  internal/logging/             the log schema
  internal/observability/       tracing and metrics wiring
config/                       checked-in configuration
docs/                         architecture, logging, config, testing
```

`internal/transport/http/` is nested one level deeper than it needs to be, on
purpose: a second transport becomes a peer directory instead of a refactor of
every module that mentions HTTP. Do not flatten it.

## Toolchain

Every version is pinned exactly. Two people minting a week apart get the same
toolchain, which is the only thing that makes the mint mark above mean
anything.

| tool | version | pinned in |
| --- | --- | --- |
| CPython | 3.14.7 | `.tool-versions`, `requires-python` in `pyproject.toml` |
| uv | 0.12.5 | `.tool-versions` |
| ruff | 0.16.3 | `pyproject.toml`, `uv.lock` |
| mypy | 2.3.0 | `pyproject.toml`, `uv.lock` |

`uv.lock` is committed and pins the transitive set; `pyproject.toml` pins the
direct one with `==`, never a range. `pyproject.toml` also carries an
**explicit ruff `select` list** rather than inheriting ruff's defaults: ruff
0.16.0 changed those defaults from 59 rules to 413, and a lint that changes
its verdict on upgrade is exactly what pinning exists to prevent.

The service starts with `python -m [[ package_name ]]`, never the `uvicorn`
CLI. The CLI configures logging before importing the app, which installs
handlers no in-app configuration can reclaim, and the service would ship two
log formats. `make run` does it correctly.

## Updating from the template

```sh
copier update          # brings in a newer Mint release
```

Conflicts are ordinary git conflicts, in the files you have edited.
`.copier-answers.yml` records the answers this service was minted with;
change an answer there and re-run `copier update` rather than editing
generated files by hand.

**Mint's version number is repo-wide.** One tag covers both language
templates, so this service's mint mark can advance on a release that
contained nothing but Go changes, and `copier check-update` will report an
update that has nothing in it for you. `CHANGELOG.md` in the Mint repo is the
only place that says whether a given release touched this language.

